<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasir al-Din (Mauritanian cleric)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1674">1674</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Nasr al-Din</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasir al-Din was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Muslim cleric of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> tribes of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. In the early 1670s he led a reform movement that overthrew the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a>, Kajoor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> valley. He was killed in battle in 1674, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/char-bouba-war">Char Bouba war</a> ended with the Hassani warriors dominant over the Zawaya.</p></div>
